---
layout: base.njk
pagination:
  data: sites.entries
  size: 1
  alias: entry
permalink: "/site/{{ entry.slug }}/"
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ entry.displayUrl }}"
  # The measured site's own mark, so a tab full of these is readable by icon
  # rather than by a truncated domain. base.njk falls back to the project's
  # favicon wherever a page does not set one.
  pageFavicon: "{{ entry.url | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}"
---

<article class="detail">

{#
	One screenshot per site page, from 11ty's service. Deliberately not on the
	leaderboards: those run to a thousand rows, and a thousand screenshot
	requests is not a page, it is a load test pointed at someone else's server.

	Not our own Lighthouse capture, though the Filmstrip section below is exactly
	that. Those frames are thumbnails sized to the emulated viewport — 250px wide
	on a mobile run — which is right for a strip of eight and too small for the
	one picture at the top of the page.

	Only above a performance score of 75, and only once there is a measurement.
	The service renders the page on a timer, so the slower the site the more
	likely the capture is of a half-drawn or empty page — a picture of our own
	screenshot timeout rather than of the site. Below the threshold there is
	nothing worth showing and a request not worth making.

	Lazy, and sized in the markup so the text beside it does not jump when the
	image lands. Decorative rather than described: what the site looks like is
	not information this page is making a claim about, and "screenshot of
	example.com" read aloud tells you nothing you did not just hear from the
	heading above it.
#}
{% set SCREENSHOT_MIN_PERFORMANCE = 75 %}
{% set screenshot = "" %}
{#
	Nothing at all when the page we measured was a bot check. The screenshot
	service is as automated as we are, so it meets the same waiting room — and a
	picture of Cloudflare captioned with this site's name claims we saw something
	we did not.
#}
{% set shotWidth = 650 %}
{% set shotHeight = 650 %}
{#
	Our own capture first, where we have one: the picture our own Chrome saw,
	taken on the same page load as the numbers beside it. Every site gets one
	from the axe pass now, so the service below is only for sites that have not
	come round for measurement since — see lib/axe.js.

	No performance floor on this branch: that floor exists because the service
	renders on a timer and photographs half-drawn pages, and our capture is a
	byproduct of a measurement that already finished.
#}
{#
	A bot check is shown, not hidden.

	It used to be suppressed, on the reasoning that a picture of Cloudflare's
	waiting room is not a picture of the site. True — but the page now says so in
	a notice above, and with that said the picture is the evidence for it: a
	reader who is told the numbers describe a bot check can see the bot check.
	Hidden, the claim was unsupported and the missing image looked like a bug.

	Only our own capture, never the screenshot service: that service would be
	photographing the site as *it* sees it, which may be the real page, and
	putting that under a notice about a bot check would contradict the notice.
#}
{% if entry.ownScreenshot %}
	{% set screenshot = entry.ownScreenshot.src %}
	{% set shotWidth = entry.ownScreenshot.width or 650 %}
	{% set shotHeight = entry.ownScreenshot.height or 650 %}
{% elif not entry.interstitial and entry.latest.lab.scores.performance >= SCREENSHOT_MIN_PERFORMANCE %}
	{% set screenshot = entry.url | screenshot(meta.screenshotService) %}
{% endif %}

<header class="detail-head{{ ' has-shot' if screenshot }}">
	<div class="detail-head-text">
	{#
		The site's own mark beside its name — the same image the tab shows, so the
		page and the tab agree about what you are looking at.

		Decorative: the domain is right next to it, and "favicon for example.com"
		read aloud adds nothing to a heading that already says example.com. Sized
		in the markup as well as in CSS so the heading does not reflow when the
		image lands, and lazy would be wrong for something above the fold.
	#}
	<h2 class="page-hed">
		{%- set siteIcon = entry.url | avatar(meta.avatarService) -%}
		{%- if siteIcon %}<img class="page-hed-mark" src="{{ siteIcon }}" width="32" height="32" alt="" decoding="async">{% endif -%}
		{{ entry.displayUrl }}
	</h2>
	<p class="detail-url"><a href="{{ entry.url }}">{{ entry.url }}</a></p>
	{#
		The same six rings the leaderboard shows for this row, so arriving from a
		table lands on the figures you clicked. Only the header summary — the
		Scores section below carries the trends and deltas, which is what you came
		to a detail page for.
	#}
	{% if entry.latest %}
	<div class="detail-rings">
		<span class="rings">
			{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
			{% scoreRing entry.latest.lab.scores[score.key], score.label %}
			{% endfor %}
			{% axeRing entry.axe %}
			{% cwvRing entry.cwvFailures, entry.cwv.assessed, entry.cwvWorst %}
		</span>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
	<p>
		{#
			Where this site currently places inside each category it belongs to —
			which is not its global rank, and is usually a much better position:
			a site can be 61st of 1,134 in one list and 1st of 19 in another.

			Only for categories that present as rankings. 11ty Emeritus sets
			`ranked: false` precisely so nobody reads a placing into it, and
			printing one here would say the thing that page refuses to.
		#}
		{#
			The unified rank first, before the per-category ones — the same board
			the home page shows, across every measured site. Plain text, unlike the
			category names beside it: those lead somewhere that lists the site,
			and this one has no page of its own to point at.

			Only for the top half. "Better than 12% of sites" is a true sentence
			that nobody put their site on this list to read, and the six rings
			above already say plainly how it is doing — this line exists to give
			credit, not to rank the bottom of the board to its face.
		#}
		<strong>Ranks</strong>
		{% if entry.ranks.overall %}
		Overall
		<span class="cat-rank" title="Ranked {{ entry.ranks.overall | num }} of {{ sites.stats.measured | num }} measured sites overall">#{{ entry.ranks.overall | num }}</span>
		{% endif %}
		{#- Biggest first, matching the chips on the leaderboard. #}
		{% for cat in entry.categories %}
		{% set g = cat.id %}
		{% set grp = sites.groups | groupById(g) %}
		<a href="/group/{{ g }}/">{{ cat.name }}</a>
		{%- if grp.ranked and entry.groupRanks[g].overall %}
		<span class="cat-rank" title="Ranked {{ entry.groupRanks[g].overall | num }} of {{ grp.measured | num }} measured sites in {{ cat.name }}">#{{ entry.groupRanks[g].overall | num }}</span>
		{#- No separator between them: each name is followed by its own rank pill,
		    and a comma after the pill reads as punctuation belonging to the
		    number rather than to the list. #}
		{%- endif %}
		{% endfor %}
	</p>
	<p class="muted data-age {{ 'stale-age' if entry.dataStale }}">
		{% if entry.latest %}
			{#- No dash and no full stop: the age is a pill rather than the end of a
			    sentence, and punctuation either side of it reads as stray marks. #}
			Data from {{ entry.latest.date | date("long") }}
			<span class="age-pill{{ ' stale' if entry.dataStale }}"><time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time> old</span>
			{%- if entry.dataStale %}, older than this category's {{ entry.staleAfterHours | duration }} freshness target{% endif %}
		{% else %}
			No successful measurement yet.
		{% endif %}
		{#
			How often this site comes round, beside how old its data is — the
			second number is what makes the first readable. Six days old is
			alarming on a daily site and ordinary on a weekly one.

			The window is the strictest across the categories it belongs to, so
			the title names which ones set it rather than stating a figure with
			no provenance.
		#}
		{% if entry.freshnessHours %}Measured {{ entry.freshnessHours | cadence }}{% endif %}
		· {{ entry.totalCount }} run{{ "s" if entry.totalCount != 1 }}
		{% if entry.windowed %}<span title="The report charts the most recent {{ entry.historyCount }}; the rest stay on disk">(charting last {{ entry.historyCount }})</span>{% endif %}
		{% if entry.firstMeasured %} since {{ entry.firstMeasured | date }}{% endif %}
	</p>
	{#
		Somewhere else to run the same question.

		These are links rather than rivals. The numbers on this page are a median
		of repeated runs on one rig held deliberately still, so that a change in
		them means a change in the site — a different question from "what does it
		score right now", which is the one anyone asks first when a figure here
		looks wrong.

		Four because they answer different halves of it. PageSpeed Insights runs
		Lighthouse on demand on Google's machines and reports this URL's CrUX field
		data beside it; `analysis?url=` is PSI's own permalink shape, so it arrives
		at a report rather than at the form. The other three are field data — Calibre
		and Treo keyed on a host, CrUX Vis on the origin behind the URL — which
		is why they take the hostname, and why they are the ones to reach for when
		the Field history table below is empty. Calibre and Treo in that order for
		no better reason than that it reads better.

		CrUX Vis carries its parameters in the fragment, so they never reach a
		server: the whole query is read by the page itself. `periodStart=0` and
		`periodEnd=-1` ask for the full history rather than a fixed window, so the
		link keeps working as months pass instead of aging into a stale range.
	#}
	{#
		Each service wearing its own mark, from the same avatar service the site
		names above use. Five links in a row read as five words otherwise, and the
		marks are how you find the one you already know by sight.

		Decorative, and named in text right beside them: "PageSpeed Insights logo,
		PageSpeed Insights" is a thing to listen past. Not lazy — this sits in the
		header, above the fold, where lazy means visibly late.
	#}
	<p class="detail-actions">
		Elsewhere:
		<a href="https://pagespeed.web.dev/analysis?url={{ entry.url | urlencode }}"
			title="Lighthouse run on demand by Google, with this URL's CrUX field data beside it"><img class="action-mark" src="{{ "https://pagespeed.web.dev/" | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}" width="16" height="16" alt="" decoding="async">PageSpeed Insights</a> ·
		<a href="https://calibreapp.com/tools/core-web-vitals-test/{{ entry.url | hostname }}"
			title="Calibre's free Core Web Vitals check for {{ entry.url | hostname }}"><img class="action-mark" src="{{ "https://calibreapp.com/" | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}" width="16" height="16" alt="" decoding="async">Calibre</a> ·
		<a href="https://treo.sh/sitespeed/{{ entry.url | hostname }}"
			title="CrUX field data charted for {{ entry.url | hostname }} — history rather than a fresh lab run"><img class="action-mark" src="{{ "https://treo.sh/" | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}" width="16" height="16" alt="" decoding="async">Treo</a> ·
		<a href="https://cruxvis.withgoogle.com/#/?view=cwvsummary&amp;url={{ entry.url | urlencode }}&amp;identifier=origin&amp;device=ALL&amp;periodStart=0&amp;periodEnd=-1&amp;display=p75s"
			title="Every month of CrUX p75s for this origin, charted by Google's CrUX Vis"><img class="action-mark" src="{{ "https://cruxvis.withgoogle.com/" | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}" width="16" height="16" alt="" decoding="async">CrUX Vis</a> ·
		{#
			Not a second opinion like the others — the same numbers this page
			already shows, in the arithmetic that turns them into a score. Which is
			the useful thing to have beside a performance figure: the score alone
			never says which metric spent it.
		#}
		{% set scorecalc = entry.latest | scoreCalculator %}
		{% if scorecalc %}
		<a href="{{ scorecalc }}"
			title="This measurement's metrics loaded into Lighthouse's scoring calculator — what each one costs the performance score">{% brandMark "Lighthouse" %}Scoring Calculator</a> ·
		{% endif %}
		{#
			Last, because it is the only one that looks backwards: everything above
			is about the site as it is now, and this asks what the page used to be.
			A site that has gone, or one whose scores moved for no reason the data
			explains, is a case where what it looked like is the only thing left to
			look at. "Elsewhere" rather than "Test elsewhere" above is what
			makes room for it.

			`/web/*/` is the Wayback Machine's own shape for "every snapshot of
			this URL" — a calendar rather than one capture, since which date
			matters is the thing you came to find out.
		#}
		<a href="{{ entry.url | wayback }}"
			title="Every archived snapshot of this URL in the Internet Archive's Wayback Machine"><img class="action-mark" src="{{ "https://web.archive.org/" | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}" width="16" height="16" alt="" decoding="async">Wayback Machine</a>
	</p>
	</div>
	{#
		After the text, not before it: the heading is what the page is about and
		should come first to a screen reader, and this way the source order and
		the stacked mobile order are the same thing.
	#}
	{% if screenshot %}
	{#
		Hidden from assistive technology and out of the tab order: this is the
		same destination as the URL linked in text directly beside it, and a
		second link to it would be a duplicate to read past and a duplicate to
		tab through. Removing it costs a screen reader nothing — the link is
		still there, in words.
	#}
	<a class="detail-shot-link" href="{{ entry.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
		<img class="detail-shot" src="{{ screenshot }}" width="{{ shotWidth }}" height="{{ shotHeight }}"
			alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
	</a>
	{% endif %}
</header>

{#
	A bot check on one of the page loads this measurement makes.

	Downgraded from an error to a warning, because it no longer means what it
	used to. A site is loaded four times here — three Lighthouse runs and the
	accessibility pass — and a challenge fires on them independently. Both sites
	this has ever applied to were challenged on the last load only, and the old
	notice told a reader that every figure on the page described a waiting room
	when the figures were a correct measurement of the real site.

	Still first among the notices, and still worth one: the screenshot, filmstrip
	and Without JavaScript section are hidden further down, which without this
	reads as three things mysteriously missing.

	A site challenged on every load has no measurement at all — that record is
	stored as an error and this page never reaches here.
#}
{% if entry.interstitial %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	<strong>The accessibility pass was served a bot check.</strong>
	<p>
		This site is loaded several times per measurement, and one of those loads
		&mdash; the accessibility pass &mdash; was answered with
		<code>{{ entry.interstitial }}</code>, an automated-traffic check rather
		than {{ entry.displayUrl }}. It was tried twice.
	</p>
	<p>
		The performance figures below are unaffected: they come from the runs that
		were served the real page. What is missing is everything that pass
		produces &mdash; the accessibility result, the screenshot and the
		without-JavaScript comparison &mdash; because those would describe the
		waiting room rather than the site.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.stale and entry.latest %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	<strong>This data is <time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time> old.</strong>
	<p>
		Sites are measured on a rolling schedule, so figures here are the newest
		successful measurement rather than a reading taken just now. Comparisons
		against other sites may span different points in time.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.redirectTo %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	<strong>This URL redirects to <code>{{ entry.redirectTo }}</code>.</strong>
	<p>
		Lighthouse follows the redirect, so the numbers below describe the
		<em>destination</em> page, not this one.
		{% if entry.redirectConfirmed %}
		The move is confirmed, so measurement has already followed it — no config
		change needed, and the history came along.
		{% elif entry.redirectReason == "temporary" %}
		The redirect is temporary (302/303/307), so it is treated as a detour
		rather than a move and history stays here.
		{% elif entry.redirectReason == "unstable" %}
		The destination has not been consistent across recent runs — that pattern
		usually means an A/B test or a geo split rather than a move.
		{% else %}
		Not yet confirmed as a move; it needs to lead to the same place on
		several consecutive runs first.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.canonicalUrl and not entry.redirectTo %}
<div class="notice notice-info">
	<strong>This URL redirects to <code>{{ entry.canonicalUrl }}</code>.</strong>
	<p>
		{% if entry.canonicalKind == "landing" %}
		The site&rsquo;s front door pointing at the page it serves there &mdash; a
		locale or CMS landing path, not a move. The origin is still the address of
		the site, so history stays under it rather than forking to a path that
		depends on where the measurement ran.
		{% else %}
		The same URL in a different form &mdash; a trailing slash &mdash; so both
		are treated as one site rather than two: one history, measured once.
		{% endif %}
		Lighthouse follows the redirect, so the numbers below describe the page
		actually served; the only cost is an extra hop on each run.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.movedFrom %}
<div class="notice notice-info">
	<strong>Moved from <code>{{ entry.movedFrom }}</code>.</strong>
	<p>
		History from the previous URL is included below{% if entry.moveAt %}, with the
		move on {{ entry.moveAt | date }}{% endif %}. A step-change around that point is
		likely the change of address rather than a regression.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.currentlyFailing %}
<div class="notice notice-error">
	<strong>
		The most recent attempt failed{% if entry.consecutiveFailures > 1 %} ({{ entry.consecutiveFailures }} in a row){% endif %}.
	</strong>
	<code>{{ entry.lastError }}</code>
	{% if entry.latest %}
	<p>Numbers below are from the last successful run, <time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time> old.</p>
	{% else %}
	<p>There is no successful measurement for this site yet.</p>
	{% endif %}
	{% if entry.consecutiveFailures > 2 %}
	<p class="small">Retries are backing off, so this site is being attempted less often than the rest.</p>
	{% endif %}
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.environmentDrift and entry.environmentDrift.suspect %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	{#
		"Than usual" would be a claim about this site's own past. The baseline is
		the median across every measurement in the project, so the comparison is
		against the fleet — said plainly, because a reader who assumes the wrong
		baseline draws the wrong conclusion from the same number.
	#}
	<strong>
		The measuring machine was {{ entry.environmentDrift.direction }} than
		{% if entry.environmentDrift.scope == "fleet" %}most in this project{% else %}usual for this site{% endif %}.
	</strong>
	<p>
		Lighthouse benchmark index was {{ entry.environmentDrift.current | num }},
		{#
			The percentage was parenthesised in an earlier version, and the reason
			survives the rewrite: a filter binds tighter than the multiplication in
			Nunjucks, so `ratio * 100 | round` rounded 100 and left the product
			alone — printing "34.300000000000004%".
		#}
		{{ (entry.environmentDrift.ratio * 100) | round | abs }}%
		{{ entry.environmentDrift.direction }} than
		{% if entry.environmentDrift.scope == "fleet" %}
			this project's median of {{ entry.environmentDrift.baseline | num }}.
		{% else %}
			this site's baseline of {{ entry.environmentDrift.baseline | num }}.
		{% endif %}
		CPU-bound metrics — TBT, TTI,
		main thread work — are affected by this and may have moved without the site
		changing at all.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if not entry.latest %}
<div class="empty"><p>No successful measurements recorded for this site yet.</p></div>
{% else %}

{#
	Built from the same conditions the sections use, held in variables so each
	one is written once. A hardcoded list would link to Field data on the nine
	sites in ten that CrUX has never heard of — a table of contents whose entries
	sometimes go nowhere is worse than none.

	Only rendered for a measured site: the branch above is the whole page
	otherwise.
#}
{% set hasStack = entry.generator or entry.host or entry.tools.length %}
{% set hasCwv = entry.cwv %}
{#
	Whether there is field data to show a column for. Not simply `hasField`: a
	site measured before the CrUX key existed has no field record on the
	measurement, and its real-user numbers reach the table through the backfilled
	week the assessment fell back to.
#}
{% set cwvSource = entry.cwv.source if hasCwv else null %}
{% set showField = hasField or cwvSource == "field" or cwvSource == "field-history" %}
{#
	An assessment CrUX actually made, as against the lab approximation the report
	falls back to. Only the former is shown as a Core Web Vitals verdict.
#}
{% set hasRealCwv = cwvSource == "field" or cwvSource == "field-history" %}
{% set fieldPeriod = entry.latest.field.collectionPeriod if hasField else (entry.cwv.period if hasCwv else null) %}
{% set fieldScope = entry.latest.field.scope if hasField else (fieldPeriod.scope if fieldPeriod else null) %}
{% set hasField = entry.latest.field %}
{% set hasFieldHistory = entry.fieldHistory and entry.fieldHistory.series.length %}
{#
	One picture is a screenshot, not a filmstrip. A page that painted before the
	first sample repeats that one frame for the rest of the strip, and the
	section would be a heading over the same image eight times.
#}
{% set hasFilmstrip = entry.filmstrip and entry.filmstrip.distinct > 1 and not entry.interstitial %}

{% set hasNoJs = entry.ownScreenshot.noJs and not entry.interstitial %}

{#
	How the scored runs were throttled. "simulate" loads at full speed and models a
	slow phone afterwards, so its figures are Simulated and anything read straight
	off that load is Unthrottled.
#}
{% set labMethod = entry.latest.lab.environment.throttlingMethod %}
{% set labKind = ("simulated" if labMethod == "simulate" else "throttled") if labMethod else null %}
{% set observedKind = ("unthrottled" if labMethod == "simulate" else "throttled") if labMethod else null %}

{#- Emoji rather than Font Awesome: the matching marks are Pro-only. Decorative — the word carries the meaning. #}
{% macro throttleTag(kind, title) %}
{%- if kind == "throttled" %} <span class="tag" title="{{ title or "Measured with the connection and processor actually throttled, so this is a slow load." }}"><span class="tag-mark" aria-hidden="true">🐢</span> Throttled</span>
{%- elif kind == "unthrottled" %} <span class="tag tag-quiet" title="{{ title or "Read straight off a load that ran at full speed, before Lighthouse modeled any throttling, so these figures are faster than the estimates beside them." }}"><span class="tag-mark" aria-hidden="true">🐇</span> Unthrottled</span>
{%- elif kind == "simulated" %} <span class="tag tag-quiet" title="{{ title or "Lighthouse's default simulated throttling: the page loaded at full speed and these figures were modeled afterwards as a slow phone on a slow connection." }}"><span class="tag-mark" aria-hidden="true">🤖</span> Throttled (Simulated)</span>
{%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{#
	The page in the order a reader works through it: what the site is built on,
	the load as a picture, the scores, then the numbers behind it — weight, then
	what the page does to the browser — and last the runs they were all read from.

	One entry per panel. Field data is a subsection of Metrics, Hygiene one of
	Page health and Field history one of the log, and a table of contents listing
	a heading beside its own subheading reads as two destinations when it is one.
#}
{% set toc = [] %}
{% if hasStack %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "stack", label: "Stack" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if hasFilmstrip %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "filmstrip", label: "Filmstrip" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "scores", label: "Scores" }), toc) %}
{#
	The Core Web Vitals verdict opens this section rather than owning one, so it
	is not a destination of its own either.
#}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "lab", label: "Metrics" }), toc) %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "weight", label: "Page weight" }), toc) %}
{% if hasNoJs %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "without-javascript", label: "Without JavaScript" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "health", label: "Page health" }), toc) %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "log", label: "Measurement log" }), toc) %}
{% if entry.showEmbed %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "embed", label: "Embed" }), toc) %}{% endif %}

<nav class="contents contents-sticky" aria-label="On this page">
	<span class="contents-label">On this page</span>
	<ul>
		{% for item in toc %}
		<li><a href="#{{ item.id }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
</nav>

<p class="muted small">Lab data is measured using Lighthouse, {{ entry.latest.formFactor | capitalize }} mode unless otherwise specified. Field data is reported from Chrome UX Report (using p75 measurements).</p>

{% if hasStack %}
<section class="panel" id="stack">
	<h2>Stack</h2>
	<div class="stack-list">
		{% if entry.generator %}
		<div class="stack-item">
			{% stackIcon entry.generator, 24 %}
			<span>
				<span class="stack-label block">Built with</span>
				{{ entry.generator.name }}{% if entry.generator.version %} {{ entry.generator.version }}{% endif %}
			</span>
		</div>
		{% endif %}
		{% if entry.tools.length %}
		<div class="stack-item">
			<span>
				<span class="stack-label block">Uses</span>
				<span class="stack-tools">
					{#- An implied tool says which tool brings it, so it reads as part of that
					    choice rather than as a second one. -#}
					{% for tool in entry.tools %}<span class="stack-tool{% if tool.implied %} stack-tool-implied{% endif %}"{% if tool.implied %} title="Comes with {{ tool.implied }}"{% endif %}>{% stackIcon tool, 18 %}{{ tool.name }}{% if tool.version %} {{ tool.version }}{% endif %}</span>{% endfor %}
				</span>
			</span>
		</div>
		{% endif %}
		{% if entry.host %}
		<div class="stack-item">
			{% stackIcon entry.host, 24 %}
			<span>
				<span class="stack-label block">Hosted by</span>
				{{ entry.host.name }}
			</span>
		</div>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>
{% endif %}


{% if hasFilmstrip %}
{#
	The page drawing itself, from the same Lighthouse run the scores below came
	from. Frames are named by a hash of their bytes, so a page that finished
	early and repeated its last frame to the end of the strip stores that frame
	once — the strip still renders at full length from a fraction of the files.

	Horizontally scrollable rather than wrapped: a filmstrip read in two rows is
	no longer a timeline, and the frames are tall and narrow enough that eight of
	them will not fit across a phone.

	Decorative, like the screenshot at the top. The timings under each frame are
	the information here and they are text.
#}
<section class="panel" id="filmstrip">
	{#
		Throttled or not, said plainly beside the frames. Lighthouse's default
		"simulate" does not slow the browser down — it loads the page at full speed
		and models the metrics afterwards — so an unthrottled strip is a fast load,
		and reading it as what a phone on a slow connection sees is the mistake
		this label exists to prevent.
	#}
	<h2>Filmstrip <span class="tag">LAB</span>
		{%- if entry.filmstrip.throttled === true %}{{ throttleTag("throttled", "Captured with the connection and processor actually throttled, so these frames are a slow load.") }}
		{%- elif entry.filmstrip.throttled === false %}{{ throttleTag("unthrottled", "Captured with Lighthouse's default simulated throttling, which does not slow the browser: the page loaded at full speed and the metrics were estimated afterwards. These frames are a fast load.") }}
		{%- endif %}
	</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		How the page drew during the measured load, at
		{{ entry.filmstrip.frames.length }} points across
		{{ entry.filmstrip.frames[entry.filmstrip.frames.length - 1].timing | unit("ms") }}.
		{#
			Every site with a filmstrip section is in the compare catalog — that
			catalog is built from the same stored strips — so the link can be
			offered wherever this section renders. `compareEligible` only decides
			what that page shuffles up unprompted; a link to a named site always
			resolves.
		#}
		<a href="/compare/?sites={{ entry.slug | urlencode }}">Compare this filmstrip against another site</a>.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll" id="filmstrip-static">
		<ol class="filmstrip">
			{% for frame in entry.filmstrip.frames %}
			<li class="filmstrip-frame">
				<img src="{{ frame.src }}" width="{{ entry.filmstrip.width }}" height="{{ entry.filmstrip.height }}"
					alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
				<span class="filmstrip-timing">{{ frame.timing | unit("ms") }}</span>
			</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ol>
	</div>
	{# The same lanes as /compare/, replacing the static strip above once the script runs. #}
	<div class="race filmstrip-player" id="filmstrip-player" hidden>
		<div class="race-controls">
			<button type="button" class="race-play" id="filmstrip-replay">Replay</button>
			<label class="race-speed">
				Speed
				<select id="filmstrip-speed">
					<option value="0.25">0.25&times;</option>
					<option value="0.5">0.5&times;</option>
					<option value="1" selected>1&times; real time</option>
					<option value="2">2&times;</option>
					<option value="4">4&times;</option>
				</select>
			</label>
			<input type="range" class="race-scrub" id="filmstrip-scrub" min="0" max="1000" value="0" step="1"
				aria-label="Scrub through the filmstrip">
			<output class="race-clock" id="filmstrip-clock">0 ms</output>
		</div>
		<div class="race-scroll" id="filmstrip-scroll">
			<div class="race-grid" id="filmstrip-grid"></div>
		</div>
		<span class="race-icon-source-finish" hidden>
			<i class="fa-solid fa-flag-checkered" width="10" height="10" aria-hidden="true"></i>
		</span>
	</div>
	<script type="application/json" id="filmstrip-data">{{ {
		width: entry.filmstrip.width,
		height: entry.filmstrip.height,
		settledAt: entry.filmstrip.settledAt,
		markers: entry.filmstrip.markers,
		frames: entry.filmstrip.frames
	} | dump | safe }}</script>
	<script src="/js/filmstrip-lanes.js"></script>
	<script>
	(function () {
		var root = document.getElementById("filmstrip-player");
		var data = document.getElementById("filmstrip-data");
		var L = window.FilmstripLanes;
		if (!root || !data || !L || !window.requestAnimationFrame) return;

		var site = JSON.parse(data.textContent);
		if (!site.frames || !site.frames.length) return;

		var grid = document.getElementById("filmstrip-grid");
		var scroll = document.getElementById("filmstrip-scroll");
		var clock = document.getElementById("filmstrip-clock");
		var scrub = document.getElementById("filmstrip-scrub");
		var timeline = L.buildTimeline([site]);
		var lane = { site: site, index: 0 };
		var now = 0;
		var playing = false;
		var raf = 0;
		var lastFrameTime = 0;
		var speed = 1;
		var follow = true;
		var started = false;
		var autoPaused = false;
		var inView = false;

		grid.style.setProperty("--cols", timeline.cols);
		if (site.width && site.height) {
			root.style.setProperty("--race-aspect", Math.max(0.5, site.width / site.height).toFixed(4));
		}
		grid.appendChild(L.renderRuler(timeline));
		lane.laneEl = L.renderLane(lane, timeline);
		grid.appendChild(lane.laneEl);

		document.getElementById("filmstrip-static").hidden = true;
		root.hidden = false;
		// Packed once visible, since it measures the rendered labels.
		L.packMarkers(lane, lane.laneEl, timeline, L.strideWidth(grid, timeline));

		function setTime(t) {
			now = Math.max(0, Math.min(t, timeline.end));
			clock.textContent = L.fmtClock(now);
			if (document.activeElement !== scrub) {
				scrub.value = String(Math.round((now / timeline.end) * 1000));
			}
			L.updateLane(lane, now, timeline);
			if (follow) L.followPlayhead(scroll, grid, timeline, now);
		}

		function tick(stamp) {
			if (!playing) return;
			var delta = lastFrameTime ? stamp - lastFrameTime : 0;
			lastFrameTime = stamp;
			setTime(now + delta * speed);
			if (now >= timeline.end) {
				pause();
				return;
			}
			raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
		}

		function play() {
			if (playing) return;
			if (now >= timeline.end) setTime(0);
			autoPaused = false;
			playing = true;
			lastFrameTime = 0;
			raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
		}

		function pause() {
			playing = false;
			cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
		}

		function reset() {
			pause();
			autoPaused = false;
			follow = true;
			scroll.scrollLeft = 0;
			setTime(0);
		}

		document.getElementById("filmstrip-replay").addEventListener("click", function () {
			reset();
			started = true;
			play();
		});
		scrub.addEventListener("input", function () {
			pause();
			started = true;
			autoPaused = false;
			follow = true;
			setTime((parseInt(this.value, 10) / 1000) * timeline.end);
		});
		var speedSelect = document.getElementById("filmstrip-speed");
		speed = parseFloat(speedSelect.value) || 1;
		speedSelect.addEventListener("change", function () {
			speed = parseFloat(this.value) || 1;
			// Rebased so the new rate applies from here rather than to the elapsed time.
			lastFrameTime = 0;
		});

		// Scrolling by hand stops the playhead dragging the view back until the next reset.
		["wheel", "touchstart", "pointerdown"].forEach(function (type) {
			scroll.addEventListener(type, function () { follow = false; }, { passive: true });
		});

		var reduced = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

		// Measured vertically only, since the lane is allowed to scroll sideways past the viewport.
		function fullyInView() {
			var rect = lane.laneEl.getBoundingClientRect();
			return rect.top >= 0 && rect.bottom <= (window.innerHeight || document.documentElement.clientHeight);
		}

		// Starts the first time the whole lane is on screen, and pauses while none of it is.
		function syncVisibility() {
			var showing = inView && document.visibilityState !== "hidden";
			if (showing && !started && !reduced) {
				if (!fullyInView()) return;
				started = true;
				window.removeEventListener("scroll", syncVisibility);
				window.removeEventListener("resize", syncVisibility);
				play();
			} else if (!showing && playing) {
				pause();
				autoPaused = true;
			} else if (showing && autoPaused) {
				play();
			}
		}

		if (reduced) {
			follow = false;
			setTime(timeline.end);
		} else {
			setTime(0);
		}

		document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", syncVisibility);
		// The observer only reports the lane entering and leaving, so scrolling decides when all of it is showing.
		if (!reduced) {
			window.addEventListener("scroll", syncVisibility, { passive: true });
			window.addEventListener("resize", syncVisibility);
		}
		if (window.IntersectionObserver) {
			new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
				inView = entries[entries.length - 1].isIntersecting;
				syncVisibility();
			}, { threshold: 0 }).observe(lane.laneEl);
		} else {
			inView = true;
			syncVisibility();
		}
	})();
	</script>
</section>
{% endif %}

{#
	Lab and field in one table, a row per metric.

	They were two tables reading top to bottom, which put the two measurements
	of LCP a screen apart and left the reader to hold one in their head while
	scrolling to the other — the comparison the page exists to make. Side by
	side it is a glance.

	Every metric gets a row, including one neither half can fill. INP on a site
	CrUX has never heard of is an empty row, and that is the honest rendering:
	the metric exists, it is a Core Web Vital, and nothing here measures it —
	which a reader should be told rather than left to notice the absence of.
#}
{% macro metricstable(rows, entryData, showField, labTag) %}
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="grid grid-grouped">
	<thead>
		<tr class="group-head">
			<th rowspan="2" class="col-site">Metric</th>
			<th colspan="3">Lab{{ labTag }}</th>
			{% if showField %}<th colspan="2" class="col-group-start">Field</th>{% endif %}
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th>Value</th>
			<th>Change / range</th>
			<th class="col-trend">Trend</th>
			{% if showField %}
			<th class="col-group-start">p75</th>
			<th class="col-dist">Distribution</th>
			{% endif %}
		</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
	{% for row in rows %}
	{% set t = entryData.trends[row.trendKey] %}
		<tr>
			<td class="col-site">
				<strong>{{ row.label }}</strong>
				{#
					A Core Web Vital graded on the lab number because CrUX has nothing
					for it. The pill beside it is a real rating on real thresholds, and
					reads exactly like the field-backed one — the word on the badge is
					what separates the measurement of this site's users from a
					measurement of one simulated phone.

					Per row, not per table: CrUX will report a site's LCP and stay
					silent on its CLS, and only the silent one is an estimate.
				#}
				{% set cwvEstimated = row.coreWebVital and row.lab and row.lab.value !== null and not (row.field and row.field.value !== null) %}
				{% if row.coreWebVital %}<span class="badge badge-cwv"{% if cwvEstimated %} title="No Chrome UX Report data for this metric, so the rating comes from the Lighthouse run rather than from real users"{% endif %}>CWV{% if cwvEstimated %} <span class="badge-qualifier">(estimate)</span>{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}
				{% if row.note %}<span class="muted small block">{{ row.note }}</span>{% endif %}
			</td>
			{#
				The value and its verdict in one cell, so the eye does not travel to
				a rating column and back to work out which number it grades.
			#}
			<td class="num">
				{% if not row.lab %}
				<span class="muted" title="Lighthouse cannot measure this in a lab run">n/a</span>
				{% elif row.lab.value === null %}
				&mdash;
				{% else %}
				<span class="metric-pair">
					{{ row.lab.value | unit(row.unit) }}
					{% if row.lab.rating %}<span class="pill pill-{{ row.lab.rating | ratingClass }}">{{ row.lab.rating | ratingLabel }}</span>{% endif %}
				</span>
				{% endif %}
			</td>
			{#
				The move since the last run, over the range across the whole window.
				One cell rather than three columns: best and worst state the ends of
				a range, which is one fact, and both are context for the change
				rather than figures anyone scans a column of. The sparkline beside
				them already draws the same shape.

				Only the change line is colored — the range is neither good nor bad,
				it is where this metric has been.

				And pink rather than red while either rating on the row says good. The
				pills grade where the metric stands; a move inside the good band is
				movement, not a regression, and red argues with the pill sitting two
				cells to the left. The sparkline beside it is softened the same way.
			#}
			{% set stillGood = (row.lab and row.lab.rating == "good") or (row.field and row.field.rating == "good") %}
			<td class="num">
				<span class="cell-main{% if t %} {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaClass(stillGood) }}{% endif %}">
					{% if t and t.vsPrevious %}{{ t.vsPrevious | deltaArrow }} {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaText(row.unit) }}{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}
				</span>
				{#
					Under the change it qualifies rather than trailing it: the badge is
					a whole word against a number a few characters long, and inline it
					set the width of the column to its own.
				#}
				{% if t and t.significant %}
				<span class="cell-flag"><span class="badge badge-sig" title="Larger than this metric's normal run-to-run noise">significant</span></span>
				{% endif %}
				{% if t %}
				<span class="cell-sub" title="Best and worst across the measured window">
					{{ (t.min if t.lowerIsBetter else t.max) | unit(row.unit) }} &ndash; {{ (t.max if t.lowerIsBetter else t.min) | unit(row.unit) }}
				</span>
				{% endif %}
			</td>
			<td class="col-trend">{% if t %}{% sparkline t, { good: stillGood } %}{% endif %}</td>
			{% if showField %}
			<td class="num col-group-start">
				{% if not row.field %}
				<span class="muted" title="Not reported by the Chrome UX Report">n/a</span>
				{% elif row.field.value === null %}
				&mdash;
				{% else %}
				<span class="metric-pair">
					{{ row.field.value | unit(row.unit) }}
					{% if row.field.rating %}<span class="pill pill-{{ row.field.rating | ratingClass }}">{{ row.field.rating | ratingLabel }}</span>{% endif %}
					{#
						A value borrowed from the origin is the whole site's number, not
						this page's — CrUX had too few samples here.
					#}
					{% if row.field.scope == "origin" %}<span class="tag" title="Not enough samples for this page, so this is the figure for the whole origin">site-wide</span>{% endif %}
				</span>
				{% endif %}
			</td>
			{#
				The bar over its own legend, which is why the header is one word now:
				each share names its band with an icon instead of the column heading
				spelling all three out. It also shows all three, where the line under
				the bar used to show only the good share.

				The icons carry the meaning for a reader who cannot tell the three
				colors apart, so they are not decorative — each has its band in the
				text beside it via the title, and the bar keeps its own summary.
			#}
			<td class="col-dist">
				{% if row.field and row.field.distribution %}
				{% set d = row.field.distribution %}
				<span class="dist" title="{{ d.good }}% good, {{ d.needsImprovement }}% needs improvement, {{ d.poor }}% poor">
					<span class="dist-good" style="width:{{ d.good }}%"></span>
					<span class="dist-avg" style="width:{{ d.needsImprovement }}%"></span>
					<span class="dist-poor" style="width:{{ d.poor }}%"></span>
				</span>
				<span class="dist-key">
					<span class="dist-key-good" title="{{ d.good }}% of loads were good"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check" width="10" height="10" aria-hidden="true"></i>{{ d.good }}%</span>
					<span class="dist-key-avg" title="{{ d.needsImprovement }}% of loads needed work"><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation" width="10" height="10" aria-hidden="true"></i>{{ d.needsImprovement }}%</span>
					<span class="dist-key-poor" title="{{ d.poor }}% of loads were poor"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark" width="10" height="10" aria-hidden="true"></i>{{ d.poor }}%</span>
				</span>
				{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}
			</td>
			{% endif %}
		</tr>
	{% endfor %}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
{% endmacro %}

{#
	The same three columns the metrics table settled on: the value, the change
	over the range it moved within, and the shape of the window. Current and
	previous were two columns saying one thing — the delta beside them was
	already the difference — and best and worst are the ends of a range, which
	is one fact rather than two columns to scan.
#}
{% macro trendtable(rows, entryData, prefix="") %}
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="grid">
	<thead>
		<tr>
			<th class="col-site">Metric</th>
			<th>Value</th>
			<th>Change / range</th>
			<th class="col-trend">Trend</th>
		</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
	{% for row in rows %}
	{% set t = entryData.trends[prefix + row.key] %}
	{% if t %}
		<tr>
			<td class="col-site">
				<strong>{{ row.label }}</strong>
				{% if row.note %}<span class="muted small block">{{ row.note }}</span>{% endif %}
			</td>
			<td class="num">{{ t.current | unit(row.unit) }}</td>
			{#
				Only the change line is colored — the range is neither good nor bad,
				it is where this metric has been. The badge sits on its own line under
				the change it qualifies rather than inline, where a whole word set the
				width of the column to its own.
			#}
			<td class="num">
				<span class="cell-main {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaClass }}">
					{% if t.vsPrevious %}{{ t.vsPrevious | deltaArrow }} {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaText(row.unit) }}{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}
				</span>
				{% if t.significant %}
				<span class="cell-flag"><span class="badge badge-sig" title="Larger than this metric's normal run-to-run noise">significant</span></span>
				{% endif %}
				<span class="cell-sub" title="Best and worst across the measured window">
					{{ (t.min if t.lowerIsBetter else t.max) | unit(row.unit) }} &ndash; {{ (t.max if t.lowerIsBetter else t.min) | unit(row.unit) }}
				</span>
			</td>
			<td class="col-trend">{% sparkline t %}</td>
		</tr>
	{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
{% endmacro %}


<section class="panel" id="scores">
	<h2>Lighthouse Scores <span class="tag">LAB</span>{{ throttleTag(labKind) }}</h2>
	<div class="cards">
		{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
		{% set t = entry.trends[score.key] %}
		{% set value = entry.latest.lab.scores[score.key] %}
		{#
			A card whose score still bands good gets the softened pink rather than
			red, the same as the metrics table below and the leaderboard trends: 96
			after 99 moved the wrong way, and the number above is green.
		#}
		{% set scoreGood = (value | scoreClass) == "good" %}
		<div class="card">
			<div class="card-value score-{{ value | scoreClass }}">{{ value if value != null else "—" }}</div>
			<div class="card-label">{{ score.label }}</div>
			{% if t %}
			<div class="card-spark">{% sparkline t, { good: scoreGood } %}</div>
			{% if t.vsPrevious and not t.vsPrevious.unchanged %}
			<div class="card-delta {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaClass(scoreGood) }}">
				{{ t.vsPrevious | deltaArrow }} {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaText }}
			</div>
			{% else %}
			<div class="card-delta flat">no change</div>
			{% endif %}
			{% endif %}
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="lab">
	<h2>
		Metrics
		<span class="tag">LAB</span>
		{%- if showField %} <span class="tag">FIELD</span>{% endif %}
	</h2>

	{#
		The Core Web Vitals verdict, above the table that now carries its numbers.
		A panel of its own restated three rows of this table and put them a screen
		apart from the rest; what it alone could say is the pass or fail, and that
		is what is left here.

		Only for an assessment made of real-user data. A site CrUX has nothing for
		gets no verdict here: its rows are marked `CWV (estimate)` and graded in
		the Lab column, which is the column that says where the numbers came from.
		A banner over those would be a claim about users nobody measured.

		Keeps `id="cwv"` so links written against the old section still land on it.
	#}
	{% if hasRealCwv %}
	<div class="cwv-verdict cwv-verdict-{{ "pass" if entry.cwv.pass else ("fail" if entry.cwv.pass === false else "none") }}" id="cwv">
		<strong>
			Core Web Vitals:
			{% if entry.cwv.pass %}passing{% elif entry.cwv.pass === false %}failing{% else %}not assessed{% endif %}
		</strong>
		<span class="muted small">{{ entry.cwv.assessed }} of 3 assessed from real Chrome users.</span>
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{% if showField %}
	<p class="muted small">
		Field figures are the p75 across real Chrome users over a 28-day trailing window
		{%- if fieldPeriod.first %} ({{ fieldPeriod.first }} &rarr; {{ fieldPeriod.last }}){% endif %}
		{%- if fieldScope %}, {{ fieldScope }}-level{% endif %}.
		{% if not hasField %}
		From the backfilled CrUX history rather than this measurement, which predates the API key.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	{% else %}
	{#
		Why the table is half the width it is on other sites. Without this the
		field columns are simply absent, which reads as a page that forgot them
		rather than as a site too quiet for CrUX to report on — and it is the
		commonest case in this corpus by a wide margin.
	#}
	<p class="muted small">
		{% if sites.cruxEnabled %}
		No field data: the Chrome UX Report has too few samples for this URL to report on,
		so every figure below is from the Lighthouse run. This is ordinary for a site
		without much traffic, and says nothing about how fast it is.
		{% else %}
		No field data: the Chrome UX Report is not being collected for this project,
		so every figure below is from the Lighthouse run.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	{% endif %}
	{{ metricstable(entry.metricsTable, entry, showField, throttleTag(labKind)) }}

	{#
		The throttled filmstrip pass's subparts where there are any: a slow load, like the Lab LCP they sit under.
		Otherwise the scored runs', read off a full-speed load before Lighthouse modeled one.
	#}
	{% set lcpParts = entry.latest.throttledLcpBreakdown or entry.latest.lab.lcpBreakdown %}
	{% if lcpParts %}
	<h3>LCP Subparts <span class="tag">LAB</span>{{ throttleTag("throttled" if entry.latest.throttledLcpBreakdown else observedKind) }}</h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		The subparts attribute LCP to your server, resource discovery, download, or rendering.
		{% if entry.latest.throttledLcpBreakdown %}
		They come from one throttled load, the same one as the filmstrip, so they add up to that load&rsquo;s LCP rather than the simulated Lab LCP above.
		{% elif labMethod == "simulate" %}
		Note that the Lab LCP shown above <em>is</em> a simulated throttled estimate.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	{#
		The unthrottled fallback will not add up to the LCP above, and the gap is usually large — 225
		milliseconds against 4.07 seconds on one site here. Both are honest; they
		are measured in different worlds.

		Lighthouse runs with `throttlingMethod: "simulate"`: it loads the page at
		this machine's real speed, records that trace, and then models what the
		load would have taken on an emulated phone over slow 4G. LCP above is the
		model's answer. These subparts are read off the observed trace, before any
		of that modelling. The same record shows the difference plainly — the
		server answered in 44ms, and the model bills 620ms for it.

		The ratio is not a constant to divide out: it depends on the request
		graph, and runs from about 10x to 20x across this corpus. So the durations
		are shown as measured and labeled for what they are, rather than scaled
		to a total they were never part of.
	#}
	<div class="breakdown">
		{% set lcpMax = lcpParts | sortByValue | maxValue("value") %}
		{% for part in lcpParts | sortByValue %}
		<div class="breakdown-row">
			<span class="breakdown-label breakdown-key">{{ part.key | humanize }}</span>
			{% bar part.value, lcpMax, "warn" %}
			<span class="breakdown-value">{{ part.value | ms }}</span>
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{#
		What the combined table above cannot hold: the same metric measured two
		ways. It sits under the table rather than in a panel of its own, because it
		is about the rows above it.

		The weeks behind the current p75 are a log of past measurements, so they
		are down in the measurement log with the rest of them.
	#}
	{% if entry.labVsField %}
	<h3 id="field">LCP <span class="tag">LAB</span>{{ throttleTag(labKind) }} vs LCP <span class="tag">FIELD</span></h3>
	{#
		Two bars against one scale, so the comparison is a length rather than a
		subtraction the reader performs. Both are widths of the larger of the two,
		which is the only scale on which "twice as long" looks twice as long.

		The lab bar is neutral and the field bar carries its Core Web Vitals
		rating: the lab number has no verdict attached — it is a simulation — while
		the field number is the one Google grades.
	#}
	{% set lvfMax = entry.labVsField.lab if entry.labVsField.lab > entry.labVsField.field else entry.labVsField.field %}
	{#
		Recomputed from the two stored millisecond figures rather than read from
		`labVsField.ratio`, which is rounded to a tenth for the leaderboards — a
		column of a thousand rows wants a figure you can scan, and this is one
		site, where 1.17 says more than 1.2. Both numbers it is derived from are on
		screen directly above, so the arithmetic is checkable.
	#}
	{% set lvfRatio = entry.labVsField.lab / entry.labVsField.field %}
	<div class="lvf-compare">
		<div class="lvf-row">
			<span class="lvf-name">Lab</span>
			<span class="bar"><span class="bar-fill lvf-fill-lab" style="width: {{ (entry.labVsField.lab / lvfMax * 100) | round }}%"></span></span>
			<span class="lvf-ms">{{ entry.labVsField.lab | ms }}</span>
		</div>
		<div class="lvf-row">
			<span class="lvf-name">Field p75</span>
			<span class="bar"><span class="bar-fill lvf-fill-{{ entry.latest.field.metrics.lcp.rating | ratingClass }}" style="width: {{ (entry.labVsField.field / lvfMax * 100) | round }}%"></span></span>
			<span class="lvf-ms">{{ entry.labVsField.field | ms }}</span>
		</div>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		{#
			The multiple, then what it implies — rather than "N× faster", which only
			reads correctly in one direction: a field number worse than the lab
			gives a figure below 1, and "0.33× slower" is not a sentence.

			And not "faster than the test says", which sounds like the test was
			wrong. It was not: it measured a mid-range phone on a throttled
			connection, deliberately. A field number better than that says something
			about who is visiting, not about the accuracy of the lab — hence
			"likely", since this is an inference about an audience rather than a
			measurement of one.
		#}
		The lab LCP is <strong>{{ lvfRatio | round(2) }}×</strong> the field p75 —
		{#
			Within a tenth either way is not a finding. `fieldWorse` is a strict
			comparison, so without this band a site a single millisecond apart would
			be told its audience is below average.

			Tested on the rounded ratio, which is the one displayed: a reader seeing
			0.9x should get the same verdict as anyone else seeing 0.9x, rather than
			two sites reading identically and being described differently.
		#}
		{% if lvfRatio >= 0.9 and lvfRatio <= 1.1 %}
		real users likely have similar hardware or network connectivity to the lab test.
		{%- else %}
		real users likely have {{ "below" if entry.labVsField.fieldWorse else "above" }}-average
		hardware or network connectivity.
		{%- endif %}
		{#
			1.5x worse or more, expressed the way round the ratio now runs: 1/1.5
			is 0.667, which one decimal renders as 0.7 — so the test has to include
			it rather than sit below a value the data can no longer produce.
		#}
		{% if entry.labVsField.fieldWorse and lvfRatio <= 0.7 %}
		A gap this wide usually means real users are on slower devices or networks
		than the simulated ones — the lab number is flattering you.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	{% endif %}
</section>

<section class="panel" id="weight">
	<h2>Page weight</h2>
	{{ trendtable(metrics.WEIGHT_METRICS, entry) }}

	{% if entry.weightByType.length %}
	<h3>By resource type</h3>
	{#
		A bar per type at its share of the whole page.

		Shares come from lib/report.js, which also drops `third-party`: it is a
		tally across the other types rather than one of them, and adding it here
		would take the bar past its own total.
	#}
	<div class="table-scroll table-narrow">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Type</th>
				<th class="col-tpbar">Transfer</th>
				<th>Share</th>
				<th>Size</th>
				<th>Requests</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for t in entry.weightByType %}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site">{{ t.key | humanize }}</td>
				<td class="col-tpbar"><span class="tpbar" style="width: {{ t.share }}%" aria-hidden="true"><span class="stackbar-part type-{{ t.key }}" style="width: 100%"></span></span></td>
				<td class="num">{{ t.shareText }}</td>
				<td class="num">{{ t.bytes | bytes }}</td>
				<td class="num">{{ t.requests | num }}</td>
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
		<tfoot>
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site"><strong>Total</strong></td>
				<td></td>
				<td></td>
				<td class="num"><strong>{{ entry.latest.lab.weight.total | bytes }}</strong></td>
				<td class="num"><strong>{{ entry.latest.lab.weight.requests | num }}</strong></td>
			</tr>
		</tfoot>
	</table>
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.thirdParty and entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.count %}
	<h3>Third parties <span class="tag">{{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.count }} entities</span></h3>
	<div class="table-scroll table-narrow">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Entity</th>
				<th class="col-tpbar">Transfer</th>
				<th>Size</th>
				<th>Main thread{{ throttleTag(labKind, "Lighthouse's default simulated throttling: the page loaded at full speed and the main thread time was scaled afterwards to a slow phone's processor.") }}</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% set tpMax = entry.thirdPartyRows | maxValue("bytes") %}
		{% for tp in entry.thirdPartyRows %}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site">{{ tp.entity }}</td>
				<td class="col-tpbar">
				{#
					Two bars, one shape. Where the request types are known the bar is
					stacked and says what the third party is; where they are not — an
					older record, or a row whose URLs the network audit did not report —
					it falls back to the plain bar this section has always drawn.

					The row's width is still its share of the largest third party, so
					the rows compare with each other exactly as before. Only the fill
					inside that width is new.
				#}
				{#
					The label threshold is the segment's share of the *row width*, not
					of its own bar: these bars are scaled against the largest third
					party, so a segment that is all of a small third party is still only
					a few pixels wide, and its own share says 100%.
				#}
				{% if tp.parts.length %}
				<span class="tpbar" style="width: {{ (tp.bytes / tpMax * 100) | round(1) }}%"
					title="{% for part in tp.parts %}{{ part.key }} {{ part.bytes | bytes }} ({{ part.shareText }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}">
					{%- for part in tp.parts %}<span class="stackbar-part type-{{ part.key }}" style="width: {{ part.share }}%">{% if part.share * tp.bytes / tpMax >= 15 %}<span class="stackbar-name">{{ part.key | humanize }}</span>{% endif %}</span>{% endfor -%}
					{%- if tp.untyped %}<span class="stackbar-part type-other" style="width: {{ (tp.untyped / tp.bytes * 100) | round(1) }}%"></span>{% endif -%}
				</span>
				{% else %}
				{% bar tp.bytes, tpMax, "warn" %}
				{% endif %}
				</td>
				<td class="num">{{ tp.bytes | bytes }}</td>
				<td class="num">{{ tp.mainThreadMs | ms }}</td>
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
		<tfoot>
			<tr>
				{#- Only the top five get rows, so the total names what it covers when that is more. #}
				<td class="col-site"><strong>{% if entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.count > entry.thirdPartyRows.length %}All {{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.count }}{% else %}Total{% endif %}</strong></td>
				<td></td>
				<td class="num"><strong>{{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.bytes | bytes }}</strong></td>
				<td class="num"><strong>{{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.mainThreadMs | ms }}</strong></td>
			</tr>
		</tfoot>
	</table>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
</section>

{% if hasNoJs %}
{#
	The same page twice: as the browser renders it, and with script execution
	disabled before the navigation. Both are captured on the axe pass, at the
	same viewport, moments apart — so the difference between them is JavaScript
	and nothing else. Neither is a full-page capture, deliberately: a site that
	renders nothing without JavaScript would otherwise produce a tall picture
	beside a short one, and the comparison would be between two aspect ratios.

	Not a claim about whether the site *should* work without JavaScript. Plenty
	of these pages are applications, where the answer is obviously no. It is the
	one thing a screenshot can show that a number cannot.

	Decorative, like the screenshot at the top: the headings above each frame
	carry the information, and they are text.
#}
<section class="panel" id="without-javascript">
	<h2>Without JavaScript</h2>
	{% if entry.clientRendered %}
	{#
		Directly under the heading, ahead of the caption and the frames, so the
		reader knows what they are looking at before they look. A blank capture
		beside a full one is ambiguous on sight — it could as easily be a load
		that failed — and the caption under it only says "Scripts disabled", not
		that the emptiness is the finding.

		A sibling of the heading rather than a member of the frame row: it is a
		statement about the section, and inside the row it was a flex item that
		had to be told to span the width. The same notice treatment the page uses
		above for stale data and redirects, because it is the same kind of
		statement — a caveat about what follows, not a caption on it.
	#}
	<div class="notice notice-warn nojs-verdict">
		<strong><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation csr-icon" width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true"></i> This page is client rendered.</strong>
		<p>
			Nothing was painted until the scripts ran. This is a web performance and robustness issue!
			{# Only {{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJsText }}% of the capture on the left is
			text. #}
		</p>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
	<p class="muted small">
		The same load, photographed twice at
		{{ entry.ownScreenshot.width or 800 }}&times;{{ entry.ownScreenshot.height or 600 }}: once with
		scripts disabled, once as rendered.
	</p>
	{#
		The share of pixels that differ between the two captures.

		Described as visual difference and not as "how much of this page needs
		JavaScript", because the two are not the same thing and the gap between
		them is large. A page that renders identically but carries a <noscript>
		banner has its whole layout pushed down by the height of that banner, so
		nearly every pixel moves and the figure lands above 50% — pypi.org is the
		case in point, and it works fine without scripts.

		Not banded green-to-red either: a high number is unremarkable for an
		application and damning for a blog, and this page cannot tell which it is
		looking at. The figure is stated and left alone.
	#}
	{#
		Scripts disabled first, so the eye lands on it before the familiar one.
		The rendered page is what everybody expects a screenshot to be; putting it
		second makes it read as the answer to the picture beside it rather than as
		the subject the no-JS shot is a footnote to.

		The figure comes last, after both, because it is the conclusion drawn from
		the pair rather than a label on either — you read the two, then how far
		apart they are.
	#}
	<div class="nojs-pair">
		<figure class="nojs-shot">
			<figcaption>Scripts disabled</figcaption>
			<img src="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJs.src }}"
				width="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJs.width or 800 }}" height="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJs.height or 600 }}"
				alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
		</figure>
		<figure class="nojs-shot">
			<figcaption>As rendered</figcaption>
			<img src="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.src }}"
				width="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.width or 800 }}" height="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.height or 600 }}"
				alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
		</figure>
		{% if entry.ownScreenshot.difference !== null %}
		<p class="nojs-diff">
			<strong>{{ entry.ownScreenshot.difference }}%</strong>
			<span class="muted small">of pixels differ &mdash;
				{% if entry.ownScreenshot.difference >= 95 %}they have almost nothing in common
				{%- elif entry.ownScreenshot.difference >= 50 %}the page changes substantially
				{%- elif entry.ownScreenshot.difference >= 10 %}parts of the page change
				{%- elif entry.ownScreenshot.difference > 0 %}they are nearly identical
				{%- else %}they are identical{% endif %}.
				A shifted layout counts as difference, so a &lt;noscript&gt; banner alone
				can account for a large figure.</span>
		</p>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="panel" id="health">
	<h2>Page health</h2>
	{{ trendtable(metrics.HEALTH_METRICS, entry) }}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.mainThread and entry.latest.lab.mainThread.byGroup %}
	<h3>Main thread work</h3>
	{% set mtParts = entry.latest.lab.mainThread.byGroup | sortByValue %}
	{% set mtTotal = entry.latest.lab.mainThread.total or (mtParts | maxValue("value")) %}
	<div class="table-scroll table-narrow">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Task</th>
				<th class="col-tpbar">Time</th>
				<th>Share</th>
				<th>Duration{{ throttleTag(labKind, "Lighthouse's default simulated throttling: the page loaded at full speed and these durations were scaled afterwards to a slow phone's processor.") }}</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for part in mtParts %}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site breakdown-key">{{ part.key | humanize }}</td>
				<td class="col-tpbar">{% bar part.value, mtTotal, "neutral" %}</td>
				<td class="num">{{ (part.value / mtTotal * 100) | round }}%</td>
				<td class="num">{{ part.value | ms }}</td>
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
		{% if entry.latest.lab.mainThread.total %}
		<tfoot>
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site"><strong>Total</strong></td>
				<td></td>
				<td></td>
				<td class="num"><strong>{{ entry.latest.lab.mainThread.total | ms }}</strong></td>
			</tr>
		</tfoot>
		{% endif %}
	</table>
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{% if entry.axe %}
	<h3>
		Axe violations
		<span class="tag">axe-core {{ entry.axe.version }}, full rule set</span>
	</h3>
	{% if entry.axe.error %}
	<p class="muted small">The axe run failed: <code>{{ entry.axe.error }}</code></p>
	{% else %}
	<p class="muted small">
		<strong>{{ entry.axe.violations }}</strong> violating node{{ "s" if entry.axe.violations != 1 }}
		across {{ entry.axe.violationRules }} rule{{ "s" if entry.axe.violationRules != 1 }},
		{{ entry.axe.passes }} passing. This is a standalone Axe CLI run and is stricter than the
		subset Lighthouse already includes.
	</p>
	{% if entry.axe.top.length %}
	<ul class="failures">
		{% for v in entry.axe.top %}
		<li>
			<span class="badge badge-{{ 'error' if v.impact == 'critical' or v.impact == 'serious' else 'stale' }}">{{ v.impact }}</span>
			<code>{{ v.id }}</code> — {{ v.help }}
			<span class="badge">{{ v.nodes }} node{{ "s" if v.nodes != 1 }}</span>
		</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
	{% endif %}
	{% endif %}
	{% endif %}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.accessibility.failing.length %}
	<h3>Accessibility failures <span class="tag">{{ entry.latest.lab.accessibility.failingCount }} of {{ entry.latest.lab.accessibility.applicableCount }} audits</span></h3>
	<ul class="failures">
		{% for fail in entry.latest.lab.accessibility.failing %}
		<li>
			<code>{{ fail.id }}</code> — {{ fail.title }}
			{% if fail.nodes %}<span class="badge">{{ fail.nodes }} node{{ "s" if fail.nodes != 1 }}</span>{% endif %}
		</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
	{% endif %}

	{#
		Best Practices and SEO are pass/fail checklists, so a score below 100 is
		always one or two named checks — listed here because the number alone
		never said which. Older measurements predate this and have no list.
	#}
	{% for cat in [
		{data: entry.latest.lab.bestPractices, label: "Best Practices"},
		{data: entry.latest.lab.seo, label: "SEO"}
	] %}
	{% if cat.data.failing.length %}
	<h3>{{ cat.label }} failures <span class="tag">{{ cat.data.failingCount }} of {{ cat.data.applicableCount }} audits</span></h3>
	<ul class="failures">
		{% for fail in cat.data.failing %}
		<li>
			<code>{{ fail.id }}</code> — {{ fail.title }}
			{% if fail.nodes %}<span class="badge">{{ fail.nodes }} item{{ "s" if fail.nodes != 1 }}</span>{% endif %}
		</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
	{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}

	{#
		The checks a page either does or does not have, kept beside the numbers
		they belong with rather than in a panel of their own — a row of chips was
		never a section's worth of page.
	#}
	<h3 id="hygiene">Hygiene</h3>
	<div class="chips">
		{% set h = entry.latest.lab.hygiene %}
		{% for check in [
			{k: "https", label: "HTTPS"},
			{k: "hsts", label: "HSTS"},
			{k: "csp", label: "CSP"},
			{k: "clickjacking", label: "Clickjacking protection"},
			{k: "originIsolation", label: "Origin isolation"},
			{k: "trustedTypes", label: "Trusted Types"},
			{k: "bfCache", label: "bfcache"}
		] %}
		<span class="chip chip-{{ 'good' if h[check.k] == 1 else ('poor' if h[check.k] == 0 else 'none') }}">
			{{ check.label }}
		</span>
		{% endfor %}
		<span class="chip chip-none">{{ h.protocol or "—" }}</span>
		{% if h.consoleErrors %}<span class="chip chip-poor">{{ h.consoleErrors }} console error{{ "s" if h.consoleErrors != 1 }}</span>{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="log">
	<h2>Measurement log</h2>
	{% if hasFieldHistory %}
	<h3 id="field-history">Field history <span class="tag">{{ entry.fieldHistory.series.length }} weeks</span></h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		Weekly p75 from the CrUX History API, backfilled in a single request.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Week ending</th>
				<th>LCP</th><th>INP</th><th>CLS</th><th>FCP</th><th>TTFB</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{#
			`rows`, not `series`: consecutive weeks CrUX had no data for arrive here
			already merged into one row. The limit then counts merged rows, so a site
			whose older weeks are blank spends one row on the gap and the rest on
			weeks that have numbers.
		#}
		{% for row in entry.fieldHistory.rows | reverse | limit(12) %}
			{% if row.metrics %}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site muted">{{ row.date }}</td>
				{% for m in metrics.FIELD_METRICS %}
				{% set v = row.metrics[m.key] %}
				<td class="num"><span class="pill pill-{{ v.rating | ratingClass }}">{{ v.p75 | unit(m.unit) }}</span></td>
				{% endfor %}
			</tr>
			{% else %}
			{#
				One cell across the metric columns rather than five empty pills. There
				is a single fact in this row — CrUX saw too little traffic that week to
				report anything — and five blank pills state it five times while looking
				like the table failed to render.
			#}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site muted">{% if row.weeks > 1 %}{{ row.from }} → {{ row.to }}{% else %}{{ row.date }}{% endif %}</td>
				<td class="muted gap-cell" colspan="{{ metrics.FIELD_METRICS.length }}">
					No data — {{ row.weeks }} {{ row.weeks | plural("week") }} under the CrUX reporting threshold
				</td>
			</tr>
			{% endif %}
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{#
		Named only when the field table sits above it: both halves are this URL over
		time, and the reader has to be told which is which. Alone under the section
		heading it is the measurement log, and a subheading would repeat it.
	#}
	{% if hasFieldHistory %}<h3>Runs</h3>{% endif %}
	<p class="muted small">
		Every stored run for this URL, newest first.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">When</th>
				<th class="col-logdots" title="The four Lighthouse categories, then violating nodes from the axe run and the Core Web Vitals verdict, as they stood at this measurement">Scores</th>
				<th>Perf</th><th>LCP</th><th>CLS</th><th>TBT</th><th>Weight</th>
				<th>Runs</th><th>Spread</th><th>Bench</th><th>Took</th>
				<th class="col-archive" title="The Wayback Machine's nearest capture to each measurement">Archive</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for point in entry.history | reverse | limit(30) %}
			{#
				A generator change is a fact about this row: the measurement below
				is the first one that saw the new tag. Rendered as its own row
				above it rather than a badge inside it, so the change reads as
				something that happened between two measurements — which is all we
				can honestly say about when it happened.
			#}
			{% for change in entry.generatorHistory %}
			{% if change.date == point.date %}
			<tr class="row-change">
				<td colspan="12">
					<span class="change-mark">{{ "↩" if change.returnedToEleventy else "→" }}</span>
					Generator changed from <strong>{{ change.from }}</strong> to
					<strong>{{ change.to }}</strong>
					{% if change.leftEleventy %}<span class="badge badge-redirect" title="This is the move 11ty Emeritus is for — measurement follows it on its own">left Eleventy</span>{% endif %}
					{% if change.returnedToEleventy %}<span class="badge badge-unlisted" title="Back on Eleventy or Build Awesome — it returns to Built Awesome on its own">back on Eleventy</span>{% endif %}
					<span class="muted small">first seen this measurement</span>
				</td>
			</tr>
			{% endif %}
			{% endfor %}
			<tr{% if point.error %} class="row-failing"{% endif %}>
				<td class="col-site">
					{{ point.date | date("long") }}
					{% if entry.movedFrom and point.via != entry.url %}
					<span class="badge" title="Measured at {{ point.via }}, before the move">old URL</span>
					{% endif %}
				</td>
				{#
					This run's own circles, not the site's current ones. A row is a
					measurement, and the point of a log is that rows differ.
				#}
				<td class="col-logdots">
					{% if not point.error %}
					<span class="recents-dots" aria-hidden="true">
						{% for band in point.bands %}<span class="recents-dot recents-dot-{{ band }}"></span>{% endfor %}
					</span>
					{% endif %}
				</td>
				{% if point.error %}
					<td colspan="8" class="muted"><code>{{ point.error }}</code></td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ (point.durationMs / 1000) | round(1) }}s</td>
				{% else %}
					<td class="score score-{{ point.performance | scoreClass }}">{{ point.performance }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.lcp | ms }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.cls | unit("") }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.tbt | ms }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.total | bytes }}</td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ point.completedRuns }}/{{ point.requestedRuns }}</td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ ("±" + point.spread) if point.spread != null else "—" }}</td>
					{#
						The benchmark index, and how far off the project median it
						sat. On its own the number means nothing to a reader — 2470 is
						not good or bad — but "+27%" explains a TBT that jumped
						without the site changing, which is the only reason this
						column is here.

						Against the project median rather than the row above, so each
						row stands alone: the step between two rows says nothing about
						whether either machine was a fair one to measure on. Marked
						when it falls outside the same band the notice at the top of
						the page draws, so a reader can see which rows that notice is
						about.
					#}
					<td class="num muted" title="Lighthouse benchmark index of the measuring machine{% if point.benchVsMedian != null %} — {{ point.benchVsMedian | signed }}% against this project's median{% if point.benchOutside %}, outside the {{ sites.benchmark.driftBand.pct }}% band{% endif %}{% endif %}">
						{{ point.bench | num }}
						{%- if point.benchVsMedian != null %}
						<span class="bench-change {{ 'outside' if point.benchOutside }}">{{ point.benchVsMedian | signed }}%</span>
						{%- endif %}
					</td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ (point.durationMs / 1000) | round(1) }}s</td>
				{% endif %}
				{#
					Only for runs that produced a measurement: a failed run says
					nothing about what was archived, so the link is left off rather
					than sending a reader to a capture that explains nothing.
					`point.via` rather than the site's current URL, so a row taken
					before a move points at the address that was actually measured
					then.
				#}
				<td class="col-archive">
					{% if not point.error %}
					<a href="{{ (point.via or entry.url) | wayback(point.date) }}"
						title="The Wayback Machine's nearest capture to this measurement — it may be far from this date, or missing entirely"><img class="action-mark" src="{{ "https://web.archive.org/" | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}" width="16" height="16" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy">Wayback</a>
					{% endif %}
				</td>
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
</section>

{% endif %}

{#
	Only where a category asks for it — see `showEmbed` in config/sites.js. The
	table of contents entry above is behind the same condition, so a page without
	the section does not advertise one.
#}
{% if entry.showEmbed %}
<section class="panel" id="embed">
	<h2>Embed this score</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Use <a href="/embed/">this web component to show {{ entry.displayUrl }}'s scores on any website</a>, updated whenever this instance re-measures. <em>Don’t forget to bundle the <code>speedlify2-score.js</code> component code into your site!</em>
	</p>
	{#
		The live one and the snippet resolve their instance URL differently, and
		the difference is the point.

		This example runs on this site, so htmlBaseUrl — the same base every other
		link here uses, correct whether served from a subdirectory or a root.

		The snippet below gets pasted onto a *different* site, where a path like
		/speedlify2/ would resolve against that site's domain. It needs the
		absolute URL, which is meta.url.
	#}
	<script type="module" src="/js/speedlify2-score.js"></script>
	<p class="embed-live">
		<speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ '/' | htmlBaseUrl }}" url="{{ entry.url }}"></speedlify2-score>
	</p>

	<pre><code>&lt;speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ meta.url }}/" url="{{ entry.url }}"&gt;&lt;/speedlify2-score&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
{% endif %}

</article>
